WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.465
Lesson 14.5.

00:00:02.465 --> 00:00:04.438
Building your own tools: reading

00:00:04.438 --> 00:00:06.830
contracts, data and simple scripts.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.285
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:09.285 --> 00:00:12.675
to read on-chain data directly and build simple read-only

00:00:12.675 --> 00:00:15.715
tools, so you're not dependent on any one dashboard.

00:00:17.015 --> 00:00:18.305
Here's the plan.

00:00:18.305 --> 00:00:19.990
First, the key ideas.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.705
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:22.705 --> 00:00:24.230
Then, your checklist.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.392
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:27.692 --> 00:00:31.047
First, Reading without code: block explorers' Read

00:00:31.047 --> 00:00:33.811
tabs show a contract's public state (e.g.

00:00:33.811 --> 00:00:37.137
a lending market's parameters, your position).

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:43.143
Next, Public data APIs (e.g. protocol analytics sites, explorers)

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:47.447
return TVL, fees, yields and prices as JSON.

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:50.298
Then, RPC: a node endpoint that lets a

00:00:50.298 --> 00:00:54.985
script read chain state directly (balances, contract calls).

00:00:54.985 --> 00:00:58.640
Use a reputable provider; reading is free or cheap.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.340
Also, Scripts that only read (never hold

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:04.041
keys) can check health factors, pegs and

00:01:04.041 --> 00:01:06.514
utilisation, and send you alerts.

00:01:06.514 --> 00:01:10.583
And finally, Rule: tools that read are low-risk; tools

00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:15.987
that sign follow Lesson 14.2 (scoped, capped, revocable permissions).

00:01:15.987 --> 00:01:18.122
Never put a seed phrase or private

00:01:18.122 --> 00:01:19.768
key in a script, a file or

00:01:19.768 --> 00:01:22.452
an environment variable on a shared machine.

00:01:23.752 --> 00:01:26.267
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:28.520
Uses only public reads, holds no

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.663
keys, and can't move funds.

00:01:30.663 --> 00:01:33.619
Worst case if it breaks: you miss an alert,

00:01:33.619 --> 00:01:36.643
so keep your other alerts (14.1) as a backup.

00:01:37.943 --> 00:01:39.403
Here's your checklist.

00:01:39.403 --> 00:01:40.938
Do it now, for real.

00:01:40.938 --> 00:01:42.025
First, I can read a

00:01:42.025 --> 00:01:44.063
contract's state on an explorer.

00:01:44.063 --> 00:01:46.134
Next, My monitoring scripts are

00:01:46.134 --> 00:01:48.182
read-only and hold no keys.

00:01:48.182 --> 00:01:51.537
And finally, Backup alerts exist if my script fails.

00:01:52.838 --> 00:01:54.048
Question 1.

00:01:54.048 --> 00:01:55.773
Why prefer read-only tools?

00:02:00.193 --> 00:02:02.374
The answer: They can't move funds, so

00:02:02.374 --> 00:02:04.038
a bug or breach costs little.

00:02:05.338 --> 00:02:06.583
Question 2.

00:02:06.583 --> 00:02:08.498
Where should a private key go in a script?

00:02:12.918 --> 00:02:14.643
The answer: Nowhere.

00:02:14.643 --> 00:02:18.573
Use scoped permissions (14.2) if a tool must act.

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:21.143
Question 3.

00:02:21.143 --> 00:02:23.118
What's an RPC endpoint?

00:02:27.538 --> 00:02:29.889
The answer: A node connection that lets

00:02:29.889 --> 00:02:32.533
software read (and submit to) the blockchain.

00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:35.078
Let's recap.

00:02:35.078 --> 00:02:38.302
Reading without code: block explorers' Read tabs

00:02:38.302 --> 00:02:40.868
show a contract's public state (e.g. a

00:02:40.868 --> 00:02:44.063
lending market's parameters, your position).

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:47.843
Public data APIs (e.g. protocol analytics

00:02:47.843 --> 00:02:51.293
sites, explorers) return TVL, fees.

00:02:51.293 --> 00:02:54.207
RPC: a node endpoint that lets a script

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:58.368
read chain state directly (balances, contract calls).

00:02:58.368 --> 00:03:01.028
Scripts that only read (never hold keys)

00:03:01.028 --> 00:03:04.078
can check health factors, pegs and utilisation.

00:03:05.378 --> 00:03:07.893
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:07.893 --> 00:03:11.066
Next up, Lesson 14.6: Searchers, keepers

00:03:11.066 --> 00:03:13.698
and arbitrage bots: how they work.
